      "writtenOn": "2016-09-08T00:19:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"\"\" range member actually be the number of bits to shift right?\"\"\"\nYes it is. if the default range argument is 262144 (aka 1 \u003c\u003c 18), then __builtin_clz(range) is 13 and data-\u003erange \u003d\u003d 2, the amount to right shift.\n\niio expect a scale parameter to process the raw data.\n\nHistorically, EC provide a range argument, where \nvalue \u003d scale * raw_data\n\nwhere raw_data is what come the EC MOTIONSENSE_CMD_DATA command and scale is:\n\nscale \u003d si_conv * range / 2^15 or 2^16\n\nrange is what comes from MOTIONSENSE_CMD_SENSOR_RANGE\nIf the raw_data is signed (accel, gyro, mag) we use 2^15 otherwise 2^16.\n\nsi_conv converts the units from the EC to what IIO expects:\n\naccelerator: G-\u003em/s^2\ngyro : deg/s -\u003e rad/s\ncompass : uT -\u003e Gauss\nbaro : Pa -\u003e hPa\n\nYou\u0027re right at this point, I can put whatever I want into the range argument. I want to put in the range the maximum value the sensor returns. For instance\naccel range is 2G or 8G, gyro is 200 deg/s, ...",
      "parentUuid": "f68dffb6_1db3f3f2",
      "revId": "1dedbed05a39b4c3698506b7c2deb9ad30605913",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
